From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yewsifofficial <yewsifofficial@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:05:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] net: stop bridging port 443, which was hiding the server from
 nn::ssl
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A debug-level Ryujinx-Nextendo trace of a working arena join and a working
Quickplay says what the game asks for before each online action:

  nifm GetCurrentNetworkProfile
  GetHostByName api-lp1.znc.srv.nintendo.net  -> 51.178.29.194
  TCP/443, ssl SetHostName api-lp1.znc.srv.nintendo.net, handshake, close

That is the game's own HTTPS, carrying its own TLS through nn::ssl, to the
account API their router fronts at the game server's address. on_connect
bridged every TCP connection to that address, so by the time nn::ssl got the
socket its peer was the bridge's own end, nx_is_server said no, and
on_ssl_handshake refused it: "handshake on socket N refused: not connected to
the game server". The request never went out.

So only the connections the game speaks in clear on are bridged at connect:
the stand-in address the login gate sets, and the server on the port the
station URL names — 51.178.29.194:60005 here, which is what our own working
log shows, so neither working connection is touched by this. The server on
443 is left plain for nn::ssl to put the bridge in its place with the name
the game set. If a game turns out to speak in clear on 443 after all (a
station URL on that port), the first bytes it writes bring the bridge in: a
TLS handshake record means it carries its own TLS and the bridge stays out,
anything else means it wants the bridge.

The same trace gives the NAT check's real ports, so the decoder reads them:
five probes to 33334, which nothing answers and whose silence is the
filtering test, then ten to the game server on 10025 and five to the NAT
check server on 10025, answered from both. It was looking for 10125, which
this game never uses.

Upstream-Status: fork-only (online play is out of nxnative's scope)
Reason: Nextendo online play for Super Smash Bros. Ultimate
Issue: yewsiff/NX2Apple (no tracker: issues are disabled on this fork)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017xdHQoEipBX2wTqN8BNnMv
---
 dm/host/hle_net.c    | 67 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 dm/host/net_online.h |  3 +-
 docs/nextendo.md     |  2 +-
 3 files changed, 56 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index 574d397..f989198 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ enum { EFD_MAX = 64, SK_POLLIN = 1, SK_POLLOUT = 4, SK_POLLRDNORM = 0x40, SK_POL
 // One table for every descriptor the host hands out, so their numbers never clash: an event descriptor (host < 0), or online a socket (host is the
 // host's own socket, always non-blocking at the host; nonblock is the game's own setting). Online, an event descriptor also has a pipe that holds
 // one byte while its count is not zero (pr / pw), so a host poll can wait on it beside the sockets
-static struct { uint64_t count; int used, flags; int host, pr, pw, type, nonblock, bridge, bport; int64_t rcv_ms, snd_ms; uint32_t ntx, nrx, nfail; int wb; } g_efd[EFD_MAX];
+static struct { uint64_t count; int used, flags; int host, pr, pw, type, nonblock, bridge, bport; int64_t rcv_ms, snd_ms; uint32_t ntx, nrx, nfail; int wb, undecided; } g_efd[EFD_MAX];
 static pthread_mutex_t g_efd_m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
 static pthread_cond_t g_efd_c = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
 static int efd_used(int fd) { return fd >= 0 && fd < EFD_MAX && g_efd[fd].used && g_efd[fd].host < 0; }   // with g_efd_m held: an event descriptor
@@ -305,13 +305,15 @@ static int sk_is_udp(int fd) { pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); int u = sk_used(fd)
 // A 16-byte datagram to or from a NAT check server, as Nextendo's responder documents it (nextendo-nncs, main.go): four big-endian
 // words, a probe {test id, -, -, local IP} and its reply {test id, the port and the address the server saw us from, the server's}
 static void nat_note(const char *what, const void *b, long n, uint32_t ip_net, uint16_t port_net) {
+    // The ports a Ryujinx-Nextendo trace of a working check shows: five 16-byte probes to 33334, which nothing answers and whose silence is the
+    // filtering test, then ten to the game server on 10025 and five to the NAT check server on 10025, answered from both
     uint16_t port = ntohs(port_net);
-    if (!nx_log_on() || n != 16 || (port != 10025 && port != 10125)) return;
+    if (!nx_log_on() || n != 16 || (port != 10025 && port != 10125 && port != 33334 && port != 33335)) return;
     const uint8_t *p = b;
     uint32_t w[4];
     for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) w[i] = (uint32_t)p[4 * i] << 24 | (uint32_t)p[4 * i + 1] << 16 | (uint32_t)p[4 * i + 2] << 8 | p[4 * i + 3];
     char d[24];
-    if (*what == 'p') NXLOG("NAT check: probe %u to %s (our address in it %u.%u.%u.%u)", w[0], ip_str(ip_net, port_net, d), w[3] >> 24, w[3] >> 16 & 255, w[3] >> 8 & 255, w[3] & 255);
+    if (*what == 'p') NXLOG("NAT check: probe %u to %s%s (our address in it %u.%u.%u.%u)", w[0], ip_str(ip_net, port_net, d), port == 33334 || port == 33335 ? ", which is meant to go unanswered" : "", w[3] >> 24, w[3] >> 16 & 255, w[3] >> 8 & 255, w[3] & 255);
     else NXLOG("NAT check: reply %u from %s: it saw us as %u.%u.%u.%u:%u", w[0], ip_str(ip_net, port_net, d), w[2] >> 24, w[2] >> 16 & 255, w[2] >> 8 & 255, w[2] & 255, w[1]);
 }
 static int bridge_fd(int fd) { pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); int b = sk_used(fd) && g_efd[fd].bridge; pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m); return b; }
@@ -386,6 +388,17 @@ static void in_to(const struct sockaddr_in *in, void *a, unsigned *n) {   // and
     memcpy(a, &g, *n < sizeof g ? *n : sizeof g);
     *n = sizeof g;
 }
+// The bridge takes a connected descriptor's place: from here on the game reads and writes plain bytes that go to the server in TLS. 0, or -1 with
+// the game's errno set
+static int bridge_take(int fd, int h, const char *sni, int rewrite_host, uint16_t port) {
+    int b = nx_bridge_open(sni, rewrite_host, port);
+    if (b < 0) return on_err(ECONNREFUSED);
+    if (dup2(b, h) < 0) { int e = errno; close(b); return on_err(e); }
+    close(b);
+    fcntl(h, F_SETFL, fcntl(h, F_GETFL) | O_NONBLOCK);
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); if (sk_used(fd)) { g_efd[fd].bridge = 1; g_efd[fd].undecided = 0; } pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m);
+    return 0;
+}
 static int on_connect(int fd, const void *a, unsigned n) {
     int nb, type; int h = on_host(fd, &nb, &type, NULL, NULL);
     if (h < 0) return -1;
@@ -394,19 +407,30 @@ static int on_connect(int fd, const void *a, unsigned n) {
     uint32_t ip = ntohl(in.sin_addr.s_addr);
     { char d[24]; BTRACE(fd, "socket %d: connect %s (%s)", fd, ip_str(in.sin_addr.s_addr, in.sin_port, d), type == SK_SOCK_DGRAM ? "UDP" : "TCP"); }
     if (!nx_dest_allowed(ip, type == SK_SOCK_DGRAM)) { NXLOG("socket %d: connect to %08x refused (not a Nextendo server)", fd, ip); return on_err(ECONNREFUSED); }
-    // Every TCP connection to Nextendo goes out in TLS, the server's key pinned: the stand-in address (the NEX login the observation gate
-    // points there) and the game server itself (the secure connection after the login, whose station URL names the server's host and its own
-    // port; the game dials it in clear, as its login went). The bridge takes the descriptor's place either way
-    if (type == SK_SOCK_STREAM && (ip == NX_BRIDGE_IP || nx_is_server(ip))) {
-        int b = nx_bridge_open(NULL, 1, ip == NX_BRIDGE_IP ? 0 : ntohs(in.sin_port));
-        if (b < 0) return on_err(ECONNREFUSED);
-        if (dup2(b, h) < 0) { int e = errno; close(b); return on_err(e); }
-        close(b);
-        fcntl(h, F_SETFL, fcntl(h, F_GETFL) | O_NONBLOCK);
-        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); if (sk_used(fd)) g_efd[fd].bridge = 1; pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m);
-        NXLOG("socket %d: NEX login through the bridge", fd);
+    // A TCP connection to Nextendo goes out in TLS with the server's key pinned, but only where the game speaks in clear. Its NEX goes to the
+    // stand-in address the observation gate set, and after the login to the port that connection's station URL names (51.178.29.194:60005 for
+    // Super Smash Bros. Ultimate); both speak plain WebSocket, so the bridge takes the descriptor's place at once, as it always has.
+    //
+    // Port 443 on the server is the game's own HTTPS, carrying its own TLS through nn::ssl: before each online action it asks the account API
+    // (api-lp1.znc.srv.nintendo.net, which their router fronts at the same address — a Ryujinx-Nextendo trace of a working arena join and a
+    // working Quickplay has it each time). Bridging that here hid the server from the handshake that follows, which then refused the socket for
+    // not being connected to the game server, so the request never went out. It is left plain for nn::ssl to put the bridge in its place with
+    // the name the game set. Should the game turn out to speak in clear on 443 after all (a station URL on that port), the first thing it
+    // writes brings the bridge in (on_send)
+    if (type == SK_SOCK_STREAM && ip == NX_BRIDGE_IP) {
+        if (bridge_take(fd, h, NULL, 1, 0)) return -1;
+        NXLOG("socket %d: the NEX login goes through the bridge", fd);
         return 0;
     }
+    if (type == SK_SOCK_STREAM && nx_is_server(ip) && ntohs(in.sin_port) != 443) {
+        if (bridge_take(fd, h, NULL, 1, ntohs(in.sin_port))) return -1;
+        NXLOG("socket %d: the NEX secure connection to port %u goes through the bridge", fd, ntohs(in.sin_port));
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (type == SK_SOCK_STREAM && nx_is_server(ip)) {
+        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); if (sk_used(fd)) g_efd[fd].undecided = 1; pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m);
+        NXLOG("socket %d: connecting to the game server on 443 in clear; whichever of nn::ssl or the game's first bytes comes first decides whether the bridge carries it", fd);
+    }
     if (!connect(h, (struct sockaddr *)&in, sizeof in)) return 0;
     if (errno != EINPROGRESS) return on_err(errno);
     if (nb) return on_err(EINPROGRESS);
@@ -506,6 +530,21 @@ static long on_send(int fd, const void *b, unsigned long len, int flags, const v
         if (!in_of(a, al, &in)) return on_err(EAFNOSUPPORT);
         if (!nx_dest_allowed(ntohl(in.sin_addr.s_addr), 1)) return on_err(EHOSTUNREACH);
     }
+    // The first bytes on a connection left plain to the server (on_connect, port 443) say whether the game is carrying its own TLS: a handshake
+    // record, 0x16 and the version's major byte 0x03, means it is, and the bridge stays out of a connection that is already in TLS end to end.
+    // Anything else is in clear and wants the bridge, which is what an nn::ssl handshake would have brought in had the game made one
+    if (len) {
+        int un = 0;
+        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m); if (sk_used(fd) && g_efd[fd].undecided) { un = 1; g_efd[fd].undecided = 0; } pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_efd_m);
+        if (un) {
+            const uint8_t *p = b;
+            if (len >= 3 && p[0] == 0x16 && p[1] == 0x03) NXLOG("socket %d: the game carries its own TLS here, so the bridge stays out of it", fd);
+            else {
+                NXLOG("socket %d: the game writes in clear here, so the bridge takes the connection", fd);
+                if (bridge_take(fd, h, NULL, 1, 443)) return -1;
+            }
+        }
+    }
     for (;;) {
         ssize_t r = to ? sendto(h, b, len, flags, (struct sockaddr *)&in, sizeof in) : send(h, b, len, flags);
         if (type == SK_SOCK_DGRAM && (r >= 0 || errno != EAGAIN)) { int e = errno; sk_count(fd, r >= 0 ? 0 : 2); errno = e; }
diff --git a/dm/host/net_online.h b/dm/host/net_online.h
index a64e8b1..13c32cf 100644
--- a/dm/host/net_online.h
+++ b/dm/host/net_online.h
@@ -11,7 +11,8 @@
 //   names     every name of Nintendo's the game resolves (nintendo.net, .com, .co.jp, nintendowifi.net) is Nextendo's: nncs2-* (Pia's second
 //             NAT check server) is their NAT check, every other one their game server. Nothing else resolves
 //   TLS       nn::ssl: a connection's handshake on a socket connected to the game server puts the bridge in the socket's place, so the game's
-//             own TLS client (NEX over nn::ssl: Mario Kart 8 Deluxe) reads and writes plain bytes the bridge carries in TLS, the key pinned
+//             own TLS client (NEX over nn::ssl: Mario Kart 8 Deluxe; the account API every game asks before an online action) reads and
+//             writes plain bytes the bridge carries in TLS, the key pinned. So a connection to the server on 443 is left plain for it
 //   NEX       (Super Smash Bros. Ultimate) the game's NEX client is pointed at a stand-in address (NX_BRIDGE_IP, observation gate OBS_NEX_LOGIN on its login job) where it
 //             speaks its WebSocket without TLS; a TCP connection there is a bridge that carries it to Nextendo's game server in TLS, the
 //             server's key pinned (Network.framework). Pia's UDP goes straight to the other players, as on a console
diff --git a/docs/nextendo.md b/docs/nextendo.md
index aa6b2ef..6637555 100644
--- a/docs/nextendo.md
+++ b/docs/nextendo.md
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ runs offline exactly as before.
 | nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's |
 | NEX login | `dm/games/01006A800016E000/patches.py`, `hle_net.c` | gate `OBS_NEX_LOGIN` on the login job gives NEX's NgsFacade a direct server address, `10.13.37.1:443`, where NEX speaks its WebSocket without TLS |
 | nn::ssl | `hle_net.c` | a connection's handshake on a socket connected to the game server swaps the bridge in for that socket, with the host name the game set; Read, Write, Peek and Poll are then the socket's. This is how Mario Kart 8 Deluxe's NEX client logs in, with no patch of the game |
-| TLS bridge | `net_nextendo_apple.m` | a TCP connection to `10.13.37.1` is a local socket whose other end goes to the game server in TLS (Network.framework), the server's key checked against the pins of `DM_NEXTENDO_PIN`, the `Host` header rewritten |
+| TLS bridge | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_net.c` | a TCP connection to `10.13.37.1`, or to the game server on the port its station URL names, is a local socket whose other end goes to the server in TLS (Network.framework), the server's key checked against the pins of `DM_NEXTENDO_PIN`, the `Host` header rewritten. Port 443 on the server is the game's own HTTPS and is left plain: `nn::ssl`'s handshake puts the bridge in its place with the name the game set, and if the game writes in clear there instead, its first bytes bring the bridge in |
 | Diagnostics | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c`, the game's `patches.py` | with `DM_NEXTENDO_LOG=1`: each NEX call that crosses the bridge by protocol and method number, a failed call's result code, what the game reports of its NAT and of each hole punch; every datagram socket's totals at its close and the NAT check's probes and replies; every failure the game records in an `ErrorResultVariant`; and gate `OBS_PIA_FAIL` (Smash 13.0.5) logs each Pia job that fails with its result. No station URL, token, name or player id is read |
 
 ## Mario Kart 8 Deluxe
